  "headline": "Speakers call for grant-based climate finance for Bangladesh",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Speakers at a dialogue in Dhaka, Bangladesh, on October 24, 2026, urged developed nations to provide climate finance in the form of grants rather than loans. During the “National Position Paper Unveiling and Dialogue” organized by ActionAid, participants called for climate justice, increased access to the Loss and Damage Fund, and the strengthening of youth-led climate initiatives ahead of the 31st UN Climate Change Conference (COP31), scheduled for November 9-20. Speakers noted that while Bangladesh contributes less than 0.5 percent of global carbon emissions, it remains among the countries most vulnerable to climate change. Shama Obaed Islam, the State Minister for Foreign Affairs, stated that funding is required to address the loss of life and suffering in coastal, rural, and urban areas, but emphasized that such funding must be provided as grants instead of loans. Islam added that at COP31, Bangladesh intends to highlight the disproportionate impacts of climate change on the health and sanitation needs of women and children. Additionally, Islam noted that Bangladesh aims to generate 20 percent of its electricity from renewable sources by 2030 and 30 percent by 2040. SM Munjurul Hannan Khan, executive director of Nature Conservation Management, stated that developing countries have submitted climate plans but are awaiting technology transfer, capacity-building support, and adequate funding from historically responsible nations. Prof Ahmad Kamruzzaman Majumder, a director at the Center for Atmospheric Pollution Studies, called for direct funding for city corporations, methane monitoring infrastructure, waste prevention measures, and grant-based municipal financing to address climate housing needs."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Ghana Health Service announces closure of type 2 poliovirus outbreak"
,
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Ghana Health Service (GHS) announced the successful closure of a type 2 poliovirus outbreak in Ghana in August 2026. In a statement issued on Saturday to commemorate World Polio Day 2026, the GHS said the milestone was confirmed through Outbreak Response Assessments (OBRA) and followed sustained immunisation campaigns, strengthened disease surveillance, and coordinated outbreak response measures. The GHS stated the achievement was made in collaboration with the Ministry of Health and international partners, including the World Health Organization (WHO), UNICEF, Gavi, Rotary International, and the United States Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC). Ghana joins Burundi, Guinea-Bissau, the Republic of Congo, and Uganda, which also closed their type 2 poliovirus outbreaks in August 2026."
    },
    {
      "text": "The GHS attributed the progress to years of investment in routine immunisation, supplementary vaccination campaigns, and community mobilisation. The Service disclosed that since introducing the Inactivated Polio Vaccine (IPV) in 2018 and adding a second dose in 2024, Ghana has consistently maintained immunisation coverage above 95 per cent. Despite the closure, the GHS cautioned against complacency, stating that continued surveillance and vaccination remain essential to preventing new outbreaks. This year's World Polio Day is being observed under the theme “One Day, One Focus: Ending Polio for Good,” highlighting the importance of community participation and ensuring eligible children receive necessary vaccines."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Transit riders and advocates claim Durham Region candidates are overlooking public transit needs",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "As the municipal election approaches in Durham Region, Canada, some transit riders and advocacy groups say local candidates are treating public transit as a background issue. These groups warn that the transit system is struggling to keep pace with community needs. The Durham regional council, which consists of elected mayors and regional councillors, is responsible for decisions regarding Durham Region Transit. Michael Gemmink, a rider from Pickering, expressed the view that candidates have failed to address these responsibilities throughout the election cycle."
    }
  ]
}
